{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CftmSywRbNxzPas5i6WRF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01ENMd3HXZkSbhQEvMcvUqKu","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"C:\\Projects\\ai-template.wt\\wi-880\\docs\\reviews\\wi-880\\003-ADJUDICATE-f75c67c.md","content":"# WI-880 amendment adjudication: 003 at f75c67c\n\nAnchor: `docs/archive/last_approved`, with system-requirements.toml copied 2026-10-09 at fb1990aa. Read with sitting 002 (`002-ADJUDICATE-2296cce.md`) and the lane's answer `f75c67cb`. That commit changed only SR-046's AcceptanceCriteria and D-003's stated premise, so the cells of SR-019, SR-020 and SR-032 are byte-identical to the ones sitting 002 blessed.\n\n- [MEANING] SR-019 AcceptanceCriteria -> the hook runs the integrity floor and secrets scan on staged content and blocks a failing commit; `--no-verify` is caught by the hosted re-run -> the same, PLUS: with no interpreter at the runtime floor the commit hooks refuse the commit, name the developer-setup install step, and neither run the floor below it nor skip it -> not the same: a new acceptance case. A hook that skipped or crashed without a 3.11+ interpreter met the old text and fails the new. BLESSED, as in sittings 001 and 002: all three hooks source `kit_python ... || exit 1`.\n- [MEANING] SR-020 AcceptanceCriteria -> scan the push range and block a secret, or PII under privacy-check -> the same, PLUS: with no floor interpreter, refuse the push, naming the install step, never scanning below the floor or skipping the scan -> not the same: a new acceptance case. BLESSED, as in sittings 001 and 002.\n- [MEANING] SR-032 AcceptanceCriteria -> the scaffolded onboard/dev-setup scripts run to a green setup -> the same, PLUS: when this repository's Windows developer setup has no floor interpreter, its attended install offers one consent-first through an available provisioner, searches again after either response, and otherwise exits with the install step; a decline runs no provisioner -> not the same: a new behaviour and acceptance case. BLESSED, as in sitting 002: its actor is named, `scripts/dev-setup.ps1` meets it, and its child LLR-326 and TC-350 were approved in sitting 002. Sitting 002's observation stands as an observation, not a return: SR-032's Requirement cell still speaks only of the templates.\n- [MEANING] SR-046 AcceptanceCriteria -> the menu, direct, discovery, readiness and delegation conditions -> the same, PLUS: a declared command using bare `python` resolves to the floor-resolved interpreter on every supported platform, and one using bare `python3` does so on POSIX -> not the same: a new acceptance case binding which interpreter a capability runs on. BLESSED. Sitting 002's return is answered:\n  - **The condition is now closed.** The pass/fail line names the two bare names and the platforms each is guaranteed on, so it no longer depends on what \"its platform provides\". This box's install-manager `python3` on Windows is now plainly outside the guarantee.\n  - **It matches LLR-047, already re-attested, act 93.** Bare `python` is exact everywhere through the interpreter's own directory on Windows and exec shims on POSIX; `python3` is exact on POSIX only.\n  - **TC-047 verifies it** on the platforms each name is claimed for.\n  - **The platform split is now stated, not implied.** The Requirement's \"on Windows and POSIX alike\" is about exposing capabilities consistently. The AC now names the one place the platforms differ, which is the honest form. D-003 argues the split: a Windows `python3.cmd` shim reopens the WI-227 argument contract, and its premise is now corrected.\n\nVERDICT: MEANING rows=4\n\nAftermath: the SR rung is released and all four rows are blessed, so the act re-attests SR-019, SR-020, SR-032 and SR-046 together, in its own commit.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2